    "product_id": "ae7da0ec-d70f-41d5-8102-d4b3c6e40664",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 201729\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1029 AM MST Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry, tranquil weather conditions with near to slightly above normal\n  temperatures will continue through the first half of next week.\n  \n\n- A low pressure system with returning moisture will yield renewed \n  rainfall chances to at least the high terrain of northern and \n  eastern Arizona during the latter half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Monday/...\nWater vapor satellite imagery early this morning shows a low \npressure system centered over Wyoming and a high pressure system \ncentered over eastern Texas. The aforementioned low has moved \nfurther north and east than this time yesterday, which has \nresulted in westerly flow aloft over the Desert Southwest instead\nof the southwesterly flow that was overhead yesterday. \nAdditionally, water vapor satellite imagery depicts another low \npressure system well off the coast of central California. This low\nwill slowly approach the coast today and eventually move onshore \ntomorrow. There will be some small increases in moisture over the \nnext couple of days (gong from less than 0.7\" today to around \n0.6-1.0\" over the next couple of days) , but the dry \nwest/southwesterly flow aloft will dominate the region and hinder \nany shower or storm chances through the beginning of the workweek.\nH5 heights will remain around 585-588dm across our region which \ngives excellent confidence that temperatures will remain steady \nstate through tomorrow. Temperatures will continue o be a few \ndegrees above normal resulting in minor HeatRisk. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Tuesday through Saturday/...\nOn Tuesday, the Pacific low will slowly weaken as it moves \nnortheast into NV/UT before moving into the northern Plains on \nWednesday. At the same time, a weak ridge over SE TX will \nstrengthen slightly resulting in a return of southwesterly flow \nover the Desert Southwest. This will result in a gradual return of\nmoisture across southern Arizona. This increase in moisture and \nsome upper level forcing from the aforementioned low, slight \nchances for showers and storms will return to the higher terrain \nareas in northern and eastern Arizona. After this low advects into\nthe northern Plains by the middle of the week, weak large scale \ntroughing will remain across the eastern Pacific/West Coast \nthrough the end of the week. By the end of the week, the high \npressure system over TX will try to retrograde over NM/AZ, but it \nwill be battling the large scale troughing over the West. \nEnsembles show PWATs staying around 1.0-1.3\" across southern AZ \nthrough the end of the week. As a result, NBM/WPC PoPs for the end\nof the week continue to show 30-50% across the higher terrain of \nsouthern Gila County and 10-20% across the south- central AZ lower\ndeserts.\n\nTemperatures this coming week will remain largely unchanged, but are \nexpected to dip a couple degrees midweek as the low pressure system \nmoves to the north of our area and cause H5 heights to dip to around \n582-585 dm. Regardless, afternoon highs will generally be in the \nupper 90s to low 100s across the lower deserts, and in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s across the higher terrain. Morning lows will generally be \nin the 70s to to low 80s across the lower deserts, and in the 60s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Morning lows should start to creep \nup by the end of the upcoming workweek (more morning lows in the low \n80s across the lower deserts). As a result, widespread minor \nHeatRisk will continue through the first half of the week with \nmoderate HeatRisk returning to the lower deserts for the latter half \nof next week as the morning lows creep up and daily normals \ndecrease. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1730Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period\nunder increasing mid and high cloud decks. Winds will generally\nfollow diurnal trends through Monday morning, with speeds AOB 10\nkts and periods of variability likely. By Monday late morning, a\nmore prolonged period with a southerly cross-runway wind component\n(for KPHX, KDVT) is anticipated to develop and may prevent the\ntypical SW/W switch Monday afternoon. Cloud bases will remain\nabove 8K ft AGL through the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nThe only weather issue during the next 24 hours will be low\nconfidence on wind directions, especially at KIPL, under BKN high\ncloud decks. Winds will generally favor SE/S at both terminals\nwith speeds below 10 kts, however, directions may veer out of the\nSW for a period this evening. Confidence is moderate to good on a\nperiod of VRB winds developing overnight into Monday morning at\nboth terminals. \n\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nTemperatures will remain fairly steady through the upcoming week\nand be around 2-5 degrees above normal. Dry conditions with no \nchances for showers or storms continue through tomorrow. Daily,\nlow-end, chances for showers and storms return to the higher \nterrain areas of southern Gila County beginning on Tuesday. MinRHs\nwill be around 10-25% today before increasing to around 15-30%\nacross the central and western districts and 30-50% across the \neastern districts for next week. Overnight recoveries will range \nfrom fair to good across the central and western districts and \ngood to excellent across the eastern districts through next week. \nBesides a slight increase in breeziness early this upcoming \nworkweek as a weather system moves through the region, winds will \nmostly follow typical diurnal patterns with modest afternoon \nupslope/upvalley gustiness (15-25 mph).\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich \nAVIATION...Whittock\nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich\n",
